item.content:encoded:
&lt;p&gt;Last week I wanted a single question answered: if a 30-day Chinese tourist visa was used on 31 December, what is the last legal day in the country? The obvious answer, "31 December", is wrong. The correct one is 30 January, and finding out why took four documents, one of which is a 2021 reply to a stranger's question on a government website.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a write-up of the search, because the shape of it repeats: the statute gives a number, the regulation gives a definition, and the actual grading sits one layer further down, where no one looks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Layer 1: the statute gives the outer penalty
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Exit and Entry Administration Law (in force since July 2013) says this about overstaying, in &lt;a href="https://www.nia.gov.cn/n741440/n741547/c1013311/content.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;article 78&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A foreigner who illegally resides shall be given a warning; where the circumstances are serious, a fine of 500 yuan per day of illegal residence, with the total not exceeding 10,000 yuan, or detention of not less than five days and not more than fifteen days.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two things are missing from that sentence. It does not define what counts as a day of illegal residence, and it does not say when "the circumstances are serious". Both matter: the difference between a warning and detention is the difference between a stamp in a passport and a stay in a cell.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Layer 2: the regulation defines the clock, in the wrong place
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The State Council regulation underneath the law (&lt;a href="https://www.gov.cn/zhengce/content/202508/content_7036507.htm" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Order No. 637 of 2013, revised in 2025 by Order No. 814&lt;/a&gt;) contains the counting rule. It is not in the chapter on stay management. It is in article 36, the definitions article at the very end:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The stay period stated in the visa means the time the holder is permitted to stay after each entry, counted from the day after entry.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That one clause produced the 30 January answer. Entry on 31 December means day one is 1 January, and thirty days from there is 30 January. The arrival day is not a day of your stay. Nothing in the visa sticker says this.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same article also settles a second trap. Article 13 gives the police counter a receipt "valid for no more than 7 days", which reads as a calendar week. The last paragraph of article 36 says every approval period and receipt period in the regulation is counted in working days, excluding public holidays. A seven-day receipt is therefore up to nine or ten calendar days around a holiday.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Layer 3: the tiers are in a Q&amp;amp;A reply
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Article 78 sets 500 yuan per day, capped at 10,000 yuan, for "serious" cases. The National Immigration Administration's &lt;a href="https://www.nia.gov.cn/Enquiry/publish/showQuestion.jsp?MZ=k0dulDWvsciKR0MEQHY4DA%3D%3D" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;published answer to a 2021 question&lt;/a&gt; fills in the tiers:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;10 days or less past the visa: warning.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;10 to 60 days past the visa: 500 yuan per day, &lt;strong&gt;counted from day 11&lt;/strong&gt;, capped at 10,000 yuan, or five days' detention.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;60 days or more, or a repeat overstay of 20 days or more: 10,000 yuan, or five to fifteen days' detention.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A visa-free overstay of up to 20 days: 500 yuan per day, with no grace period mentioned.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The "counted from day 11" parenthetical is doing a lot of work. It means a 12-day overstay costs 500 yuan, not 6,000. It also means the cap arrives at day 30, and every day from 31 to 60 is free at the margin, because the total cannot exceed 10,000 yuan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I found no other official document that prints those brackets. The statute does not, the regulation does not, and the city-level service guides I read only reproduce the statute's sentence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The code I used to check my own arithmetic
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Dates and money are where I make mistakes, so I wrote both calculations as assertions rather than trusting a mental count:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;datetime&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timedelta&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;last_legal_day&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;stay_days&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# art. 36(3): the stay is counted from the day after entry&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;timedelta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;stay_days&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;overstay_fine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;visa_free&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;False&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;tuple&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"""Published grading: returns (fine, sanction)."""&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;and&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;visa_free&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"warning"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;visa_free&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;500&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fine or 5 days detention"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"10,000 or 5-15 days detention"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;60&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;500&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fine or 5 days detention"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"10,000 or 5-15 days detention"&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;last_legal_day&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;12&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;31&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2027&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;overstay_fine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"warning"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;overstay_fine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;11&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;500&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;overstay_fine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10_000&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;overstay_fine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;45&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10_000&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# cap, not 17,500&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;overstay_fine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;visa_free&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10_000&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The last two asserts are where the published text and intuition part company. In the visa band the fine is capped from day one, so 20 days hits the ceiling. In the visa-free band there is no ten-day grace written in, so the ceiling arrives earlier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the documents could not answer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three questions stayed open, and I left them open in the write-up rather than filling them with a plausible guess:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Applying late.&lt;/strong&gt; The law says apply at least seven days before the stay ends. It does not say what a counter does with an application filed with three days left.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Whether the ceilings are granted.&lt;/strong&gt; The service guide lists a maximum extension per visa category, 30 days for tourist visas and 180 for several business and family categories. Nothing published says how often the maximum is granted.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;How the grading lands in practice.&lt;/strong&gt; No public statistics on warnings, fines and detention exist, so the tiers describe a standard, not outcomes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I would tell the next person doing this
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Read the definitions article first. In Chinese administrative texts the definitions sit at the end of the regulation, and they carry the rules that change the arithmetic: what a day is, which days count, whether "7 days" means working days. The operative articles assume you have already read them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also check the revision date before citing an article number. The regulation was revised in 2025, and the 2025 amendment added one visa category and renumbered items. A blog post citing the 2013 numbering will look right and be wrong.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The full write-up, with the tables and the article-by-article citations, is at &lt;a href="https://notebookfield.com/posts/china-visa-stay-clock-and-overstay/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;notebookfield.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Drafted with an AI assistant from my own reading of the primary documents; every clause number and link here was checked against the source.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Adding a Structured Metadata Manifest to Content‑Automation for Reliable CI Publishing
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; I introduced a &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; file per content batch so the CI pipeline can discover, validate, and route posts without relying on fragile folder naming. This change makes the automation deterministic and ready for multilingual scaling.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our &lt;code&gt;content-automation&lt;/code&gt; repo drives a GitHub Actions workflow that reads markdown files, builds front‑matter, and pushes the payload to Dev.to, Bluesky, and a static site generator. The workflow used a &lt;strong&gt;directory‑scan&lt;/strong&gt; approach:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# pseudo‑code used in the old pipeline&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; file &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; content/**/post.md&lt;span class="o"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;do&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# infer language from folder name, e.g. content/en/...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# infer date from folder hierarchy, e.g. content/2026/11/15/&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;done&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;When we added a new language (&lt;code&gt;fr&lt;/code&gt;) and started grouping posts by &lt;strong&gt;topic&lt;/strong&gt; instead of date, the scanner started missing files and, worse, mis‑routing posts to the wrong platform. The error we saw in the Action logs was:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Error: Unable to determine language for ./content/2026/11/15/post.md
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The root cause was that the pipeline &lt;strong&gt;assumed&lt;/strong&gt; a fixed folder structure (&lt;code&gt;content/&amp;lt;year&amp;gt;/&amp;lt;month&amp;gt;/&amp;lt;day&amp;gt;/&amp;lt;lang&amp;gt;/post.md&lt;/code&gt;). Any deviation broke the discovery step, leading to silent drops and duplicated posts.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


What I Tried First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first instinct was to &lt;strong&gt;normalize the folder names&lt;/strong&gt; on the fly:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// attempt 1 – normalizePath()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;normalizePath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;parts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;split&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'/'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// try to guess language by matching known codes&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;parts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;find&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;part&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'en'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'es'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'de'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;part&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// fallback to default 'en'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'en'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;parts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;slice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'-'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I added this helper to the loader script and ran the workflow locally. It worked for existing content, but when a post was placed directly under &lt;code&gt;content/2026/11/15/&lt;/code&gt; without a language subfolder, the function defaulted to &lt;code&gt;'en'&lt;/code&gt;. That caused &lt;strong&gt;incorrect language tags&lt;/strong&gt; on the published article, which we caught only after the post went live.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The approach also introduced a &lt;strong&gt;maintenance burden&lt;/strong&gt;: every time we added a new language or changed the folder hierarchy we had to update the heuristic list. It was clear we needed a more explicit contract between the content and the automation.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Define a JSON manifest per batch
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I created a new file &lt;code&gt;content/2026/11/15/content-automation/metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;. The diff from the commit looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "repo": "content-automation",&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "date": "2026-11-15",&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "languages": [&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+    "es",&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+    "en"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  ],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "topics": [&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+    "Productivity"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  ],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "commits": 4,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "pull_requests": null&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The manifest captures everything the pipeline needs:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Field&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Why it matters&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;repo&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Allows the same script to be reused across multiple repos.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;date&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Single source of truth for the batch date (used for front‑matter).&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;languages&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Explicit list; no guessing.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;topics&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Drives tag generation on each platform.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;commits&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;pull_requests&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Optional metrics for analytics dashboards.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Update the loader (&lt;code&gt;src/loader.js&lt;/code&gt;)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I added a small module that reads the manifest, validates it, and returns a typed object. The file now lives at &lt;code&gt;src/loader.js&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/loader.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'fs'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'path'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadMetadata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;batchPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;batchPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'metadata.json'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;existsSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`Metadata file missing at ${metaPath}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'utf-8'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`Invalid JSON in ${metaPath}: ${e.message}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Minimal validation – keep it lightweight for CI&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;required&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'repo'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'date'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'languages'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'topics'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;required&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`Metadata validation error: missing "${key}"`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Export for the workflow script&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;exports&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;loadMetadata&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The CI script (&lt;code&gt;.github/workflows/publish.yml&lt;/code&gt;) now calls this loader:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# .github/workflows/publish.yml (excerpt)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Load batch metadata&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;node -e "const {loadMetadata}=require('./src/loader');&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;const meta=loadMetadata('content/2026/11/15/content-automation');&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;console.log(JSON.stringify(meta));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;" &amp;gt; /tmp/meta.json
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Set output&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;echo "metadata=$(cat /tmp/meta.json)" &amp;gt;&amp;gt; $GITHUB_OUTPUT&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


3. Wire the manifest into the publishing logic
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;code&gt;src/publish.js&lt;/code&gt; I replaced the heuristic language detection with the explicit list from the manifest:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/publish.js (excerpt)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;loadMetadata&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'./loader'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadMetadata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;BATCH_PATH&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;languages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;forEach&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mdPath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;BATCH_PATH&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${lang}.md`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;existsSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;mdPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;warn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`⚠️ No markdown for language "${lang}" – skipping`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;mdPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'utf-8'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;frontMatter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`---
title: "My Post"
date: ${meta.date}
tags: ${meta.topics.join(', ')}
lang: ${lang}
---\n`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Build payload for each platform&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;frontMatter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// sendPayload(payload, platform);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


4. Add a tiny schema test (future‑proofing)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;While not required for the first release, I committed a&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Part of my &lt;a href="https://dev.to/zaerohell"&gt;Build in Public&lt;/a&gt; series — sharing the real process of building SaaS projects from Playa del Carmen, México.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Repo: &lt;code&gt;zaerohell/content-automation&lt;/code&gt; · 2026-11-16&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;#playadev #buildinpublic&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


HyperDX API keys put observability access behind a team boundary
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;HyperDX is an observability platform for logs, metrics, traces, and session replays. Its team settings hold API access keys, and the project's MCP documentation describes Bearer token authentication for an agent-facing endpoint. A HyperDX instance can therefore hold two kinds of sensitive authority: the telemetry it stores and the tokens that can query or manage that telemetry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A count-only ZoomEye probe on 18 November 2026 used page 1, one result per page, and &lt;code&gt;sub_type=all&lt;/code&gt;. &lt;code&gt;app="HyperDX"&lt;/code&gt; returned 0, &lt;code&gt;title="HyperDX"&lt;/code&gt; returned 157, and &lt;code&gt;http.body="HyperDX"&lt;/code&gt; returned 656. These are field matches. They do not show how many production teams use the software, and they do not prove that an API key is exposed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Separate ingestion from management
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Telemetry ingestion and management access have different risk. An ingestion key that only writes logs is not equivalent to a personal key that can query dashboards, modify alerts, or use the MCP server. The HyperDX documentation distinguishes a personal API access key used for the external API and MCP from the credential used to send OTLP data. Inventory those credentials separately and record their scope, owner, creation time, expiry, and rotation path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project changelog also describes team API keys with descriptions and expiry and the ability to rotate personal access keys. Those features reduce the lifetime problem only when operators use them. A long-lived key generated once at account creation and never rotated remains a durable credential, even if the product now supports replacement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The 157 title matches are the more focused control-plane population. The 656 body matches include documentation and discussions that mention HyperDX. A query that finds the brand in a page title does not tell whether the API is exposed, whether login is enabled, or whether the instance belongs to an organization.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Review the exposed routes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Confirm the front-end host, API host, ingestion route, and any &lt;code&gt;/api/mcp&lt;/code&gt; endpoint. The MCP documentation says not every deployment exposes the backend directly, so use the deployment's actual proxy and route configuration. Put the management API and MCP endpoint behind the same identity and network policy as the team interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Review team membership and sign-in. If the instance uses local accounts, enforce individual identities and remove departed users. If it uses OIDC, verify the team and group mapping and test what happens when a user is removed. Check whether a browser session can be reused after removal and whether API keys survive account changes. Those are separate controls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Audit key usage at the API and proxy layers. Alert on keys used from unexpected networks, queries over unusually long time ranges, new dashboard or alert changes, and calls to the MCP endpoint from hosts that are not registered automation systems. Do not put raw keys in dashboards, alerts, query labels, or support tickets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Make the count useful
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use ZoomEye to find branded pages outside the central inventory, then verify ownership with approved requests. Record the instance URL, team, authentication mode, API route, MCP exposure, and key owners. A title match without an owner is a lead. A managed service that the platform team already knows about is not a new finding.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Compare observations over time to catch a new deployment or a proxy change. A drop in title matches can reflect a login page with a generic title rather than a shutdown. The analyst should preserve the exact field query and recheck the host before changing the asset ledger.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;HyperDX is valuable because it brings operational evidence into one place. That concentration makes key scope and team boundaries part of the security review. The external count identifies candidate services. Key inventory and route checks determine whether the observability plane is controlled.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/hyperdxio/hyperdx/blob/main/MCP.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;HyperDX MCP authentication documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/hyperdxio/hyperdx" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;HyperDX project documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.zoomeye.ai/searchResult?q=dGl0bGU9Ikh5cGVyRFgi" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ZoomEye title-field measurement&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"The internet is down" covers six different problems, and only one of them deserves the word global. Deciding which one you're in is the first job of an incident, because the fix, the people you call, and the customer message all differ by class.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F2oiuqazjvzcnvcder4eo.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F2oiuqazjvzcnvcder4eo.png" alt=" " width="799" height="449"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Local ISP or customer-premises failure.&lt;/strong&gt; Your office fiber, a modem, an ONT, a regional ISP footprint. Everything is unreachable from one location while the same services work from a phone on cellular. Most common class by a wide margin.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Regional carrier or backbone disruption.&lt;/strong&gt; A transit provider, subsea cable or metro ring loses capacity. Multiple independent networks in one geography lose the same paths.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;DNS or BGP incident.&lt;/strong&gt; A bad route announcement or a resolver failure makes healthy services unreachable for millions of users with no intrusion anywhere.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cloud or SaaS dependency outage.&lt;/strong&gt; One provider's region, AZ or control plane fails and every product built on it degrades at once.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;DDoS or confirmed cyberattack.&lt;/strong&gt; Resource exhaustion by traffic. An availability attack, which is a different thing from an intrusion that compromises systems or data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Coincident reports that only look global.&lt;/strong&gt; Several unrelated incidents in the same hour, user-report maps light up, social media supplies the word "global."
That last class is underrated and it's usually the answer. Thousands of providers run incidents every day; on any given afternoon several overlap. A check across nine provider status pages on 17 November turned up exactly that shape — Cloudflare with R2 errors from South America and some AI models, Apple with iCloud sign-in problems, Twilio with eight carrier-specific incidents across seven countries, OpenAI with a delayed Costs API. Microsoft, GitHub and Google Cloud reported nothing; AWS showed two long-running regional issues in UAE and Bahrain.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every one bounded by product, region or carrier. None attributed to malicious activity. A genuine global outage needs a &lt;em&gt;shared&lt;/em&gt; layer to fail: a backbone, a major DNS or BGP path, or a cloud control plane the others depend on.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The diagnosis matrix
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Work it top to bottom. Starting at "have we been hacked" and working backwards burns the first hour chasing the rarest explanation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Symptom&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Likely layer&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;First check (&amp;lt;5 min)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What confirms it&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What isn't enough&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Unreachable from one office; cellular works&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Local ISP / CPE&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Modem or ONT lights; ISP status page; a second site on the same ISP&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ISP notice naming your footprint&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A spike on a national outage map&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Several sites in one region lose the same destinations&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Regional carrier&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Traceroute from two providers in the region&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Multiple independent networks losing the same paths; route telemetry&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A list of unrelated SaaS apps being slow&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Sites "won't load" but IPs ping; SERVFAIL or NXDOMAIN&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;DNS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Query a second resolver; test authoritative servers for your domains&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Resolver or authoritative failures plus a provider acknowledgement&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A user saying "the website is down"&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Specific destinations unreachable from many networks; paths change abruptly&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;BGP / routing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Looking-glass or route-collector check for your prefixes and theirs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Abnormal announcements or withdrawals plus an operator statement&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Social posts speculating about a hack&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One vendor's products degrade together; your infra is healthy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cloud / SaaS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Provider status page and your tenant's service-health view&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Provider incident naming region, service, impact&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Downdetector alone&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Edge floods with traffic; latency climbs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;DDoS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Edge and WAF dashboards; provider DDoS notice&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Traffic evidence plus provider or security confirmation and a mitigation record&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Outage size or suddenness&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Unexpected admin activity or config changes alongside the outage&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Intrusion&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Identity logs, change audit, EDR; open a security incident&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Forensic evidence of compromised accounts, or a provider disclosure&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Any loss of availability by itself&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Outage and security incident are different words
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An outage is loss of availability. A security incident is evidence that confidentiality, integrity or control was compromised.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Outage&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Security incident&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;What failed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reachability or capacity&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Trust: an account, system, dataset or control plane&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Who declares it&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Provider status page, NOC, your SRE lead&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Your security team, a provider disclosure, or a regulator&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Evidence&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Status incident, route telemetry, resolver errors&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Forensic artifacts, compromised credentials, attacker activity in logs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;What ends it&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Service restored, confirmed by synthetic checks&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Containment, eradication, recovery, written root cause&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Customer message&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;"Provider X is degraded; here's the workaround and next update"&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Legal and compliance review before any statement&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A DDoS sits in both columns — an attack by intent, an outage by effect. And a vulnerability is a weakness, not an incident; it becomes one when someone exploits it. Treat an unpatched edge device as a reason to investigate during an outage, not as a reason to announce an attack.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rule: use the word "attack" only after a responsible primary source confirms it. Everything before that is an outage of unknown cause, and that phrase is fine to put in a customer notice.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


First 15 minutes, first hour, first four hours
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;0–15 min — is this us, our ISP, or a provider?&lt;/strong&gt; Confirm the symptom from a second network. Check your ISP's status page and your own synthetic monitors. Identify one known-good path for the most critical flow. Open a channel and name an incident lead. The output is a scope statement with the evidence behind it, not a cause.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;15–60 min — which layer, and what do we tell customers?&lt;/strong&gt; Run the matrix. Pull the dependency map and list every customer-facing flow touching the affected layer. Publish one message with known scope, workaround, and next update time. Route everything else through the incident lead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1–4 hours — which fallback, and what do we preserve?&lt;/strong&gt; Activate only fallbacks tested in the last quarter. An untested failover at hour two turns one outage into two. Preserve logs, route snapshots, DNS query logs and edge captures with timestamps, because if the classification later moves toward attack, the logs that would settle it have usually rotated away.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Day 1–2.&lt;/strong&gt; Rebuild the timeline from evidence rather than memory. Review which flows failed, which fallbacks worked, which didn't exist.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How much redundancy is worth buying
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Enough for the flows whose failure costs more than the redundancy. This runs against the commercial interest of anyone selling resilience work, so it's worth saying plainly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where independence pays&lt;/strong&gt;: a second ISP on a different physical path at a site that can't run on cellular; secondary DNS at a different provider with the zone actually replicated and tested; a provider-independent path for the one or two flows whose outage starts a regulatory clock or costs measurable revenue within the hour.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where it costs more than it saves&lt;/strong&gt;: two clouds for a workload that tolerates four hours down; dual-vendor SaaS for email or chat; any failover you can't test quarterly. Every added provider is also added surface — a second identity integration, a second credential set, a second patch cycle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Five decisions cover most of it: map dependencies before buying anything; define a known-good path per critical flow and who may switch to it; delete untested failovers from the runbook until they're exercised; retain evidence long enough to classify an incident days later; and decide in advance which status sources your incident lead trusts, so nobody classifies an incident from a social feed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A team that gets those right has most of the resilience it will ever use. A team that buys a second cloud before doing the dependency map has bought a second set of problems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Full write-up — the November 17 provider-by-provider check, the full matrix, and the industry dependency chains: &lt;a href="https://teamvoy.com/blog/global-internet-outage/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;teamvoy.com/blog/global-internet-outage&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Written by the Teamvoy team. More engineering writing at &lt;a href="https://teamvoy.com/blog" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;teamvoy.com/blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a rewrite changes "I cannot take on the extra 6 reports this month" into "I can take on the extra 6 reports this month," every name, number, and date survives. The checker sees all anchors present. The refusal has become a commitment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That gap between literal token retention and semantic fidelity is the whole reason this fixture exists.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The fixture
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Download &lt;code&gt;cases.json&lt;/code&gt; from &lt;a href="https://hemmingway.io/downloads/email-rewrite-fidelity/cases.json" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;hemmingway.io/downloads/email-rewrite-fidelity/cases.json&lt;/a&gt;. It contains six synthetic English emails, each with:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;id&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;source&lt;/code&gt; (the original message)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;anchors&lt;/code&gt; (named literal strings that must survive)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;manual_invariants&lt;/code&gt; (conditions, negations, actor assignments, and tentativeness a reviewer must verify by hand)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are not customer emails. No model has been run against them. They exist to teach the checking method.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


One concrete case: refusal and alternative
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Take &lt;code&gt;refusal_and_alternative&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hi Sam, I cannot take on the extra 6 reports this month. I can review 2 outlines next month, but I have not agreed to write them. Would that help? Thanks, Arjun.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Anchors: &lt;code&gt;Sam&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;6&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;2&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Arjun&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the deliberately corrupted offline checker control, not an AI output:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hi Sam, I can take on the extra 6 reports this month. I can review 2 outlines next month, but I have agreed to write them. Would that help? Thanks, Arjun.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Construction: &lt;code&gt;source.replace('cannot take','can take').replace('have not agreed','have agreed')&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every anchor survives. The checker returns &lt;code&gt;needs_manual_semantic_review&lt;/code&gt;. But two obligations flipped:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The refusal ("cannot take on") became a commitment ("can take on").&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The non-agreement ("have not agreed") became an agreement ("have agreed").&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a refusal/negation/commitment inversion, not a reversal of shipment conditionality. The literal tokens are identical except for the negation words.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The checker
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;check_fidelity.py&lt;/code&gt; is a Python standard-library script. It reads an output JSON file (a flat object mapping each case ID to the rewritten string) and checks whether every anchor appears as a whole token in the normalized output text.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;missing&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;anchor&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;anchor&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'anchors'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;re&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;search&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'(?&amp;lt;!\w)'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;re&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;escape&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;normalized&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;anchor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'(?!\w)'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Statuses:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;missing_output&lt;/code&gt; — the output field is empty or absent.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;literal_check_failed&lt;/code&gt; — one or more anchors are absent.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;needs_manual_semantic_review&lt;/code&gt; — all anchors present. &lt;strong&gt;This is not a pass.&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Exit code: &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt; means every case reached &lt;code&gt;needs_manual_semantic_review&lt;/code&gt;. Exit &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt; means at least one case failed a literal check or has no output.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Running it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Save all three files in one directory first:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;cases.json&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;check_fidelity.py&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;outputs.example.json&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;cp outputs.example.json my_outputs.json
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# edit my_outputs.json with your rewritten strings&lt;/span&gt;

python3 check_fidelity.py my_outputs.json
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The checker prints a JSON object with a &lt;code&gt;notice&lt;/code&gt; field and per-case results. The notice reads: "Literal survival is not semantic correctness. No automatic model pass is assigned."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The tested semantic control
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The exact tested control JSON is available separately at &lt;a href="https://hemmingway.io/downloads/email-rewrite-fidelity/tested-semantic-control.json" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tested-semantic-control.json&lt;/a&gt;. It is not one of the six source cases, not a model-generated rewrite, and not an added benchmark result. It documents the &lt;code&gt;refusal_and_alternative&lt;/code&gt; inversion shown above, with construction, observed status, and the explicit note that &lt;code&gt;model_generated&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why human review is still required
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The five control checks validate the checker's logic, not any model. No model request has been sent for this dataset. No accuracy percentage, ranking, or zero-error claim can be derived from these fixtures.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What the manual rubric catches:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Conditions flipped to commitments&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Negation dropped ("cannot" becomes "can"; "not agreed" becomes "agreed")&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Actor changes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Certainty inflation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Attachment claims&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Adapting the fixture
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The structure is portable. Replace the six sources with your own authorized synthetic or nonconfidential messages. Keep the same JSON shape: &lt;code&gt;id&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;source&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;anchors&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;manual_invariants&lt;/code&gt;. The checker will work unchanged. Freeze your inputs and settings before running any evaluation. Preserve every output, including failures. Do not cherry-pick the best rewrites for a reported metric.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Limitations
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Six English synthetic cases. Not representative of all real email behavior.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Literal checks are not semantic correctness.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No model benchmark has run. No consumer tool was tested.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No automatic sending is involved.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No legal or security guarantee.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Technical resources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://hemmingway.io/downloads/email-rewrite-fidelity/cases.json" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;cases.json&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://hemmingway.io/downloads/email-rewrite-fidelity/check_fidelity.py" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;check_fidelity.py&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://hemmingway.io/downloads/email-rewrite-fidelity/outputs.example.json" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;outputs.example.json&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://hemmingway.io/downloads/email-rewrite-fidelity/tested-semantic-control.json" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tested-semantic-control.json&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Disclosures
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The publisher is Hemmingway, a writing-model company. This article was drafted by Hemmingway-1 on Medium with editorial review and local checker validation. No independent research or peer review was performed. The editorial drafting process is distinct from API or consumer model evaluation; nothing in this article tests the consumer rewriter or any model's output.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/mlh-hackathon"&gt;MLH x DEV Writing Challenge&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How many tabs did you open the last time you prepared for a conference?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now picture a business angel preparing for Slush. One calendar. No analyst. 2,989 attendees on Cala's list. Five tabs per startup: website, LinkedIn, a funding database, search, news.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's about 15,000 tabs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And after all that, you get to the only part that matters, the decision, already tired.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We spent 48 hours at Junction 2026 making that number zero. We finished &lt;strong&gt;third in Cala's track, Make Unicorns Real&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This post covers three things:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What we built&lt;/strong&gt;, and the one rule that keeps it honest.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The trick that took us from 5 to 27 trusted startups&lt;/strong&gt; without adding a single data source.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What breaks&lt;/strong&gt; when you build a real-time product on a young graph API, and how we worked around it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;First point: the product.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Grabbit&lt;/strong&gt; is a proactive research agent for investors who decide alone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You give it a vague thesis: &lt;em&gt;"AI startups at pre-seed and seed."&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It asks two or three questions, each backed by numbers from the candidate set.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You name the investors you already trust.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It walks their funding rounds in Cala's graph, keeps the startups attending Slush, and ranks them by how close they sit to the people you respect.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You correct the report in plain words, &lt;em&gt;"shorter, one line per startup"&lt;/em&gt;, and it rewrites itself.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;When you leave, it keeps watching. If a startup on your shortlist moves, you get an alert.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Grabbit never tells you what to buy. The investor decides.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the whole idea seen from the investor's side:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What the investor wants&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What usually gets in the way&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What Grabbit does&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A shortlist worth their time&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Too many candidates (2,989)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Ranks by proximity to investors they already trust&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;To believe the numbers&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Data with no clear source&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A source badge on every fact, from Cala's &lt;code&gt;source_rank&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;To get there fast&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Hours of manual research&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A first report from a single chat turn, then alerts&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;To do less work&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~15,000 tabs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No tabs. You talk to the report&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Second point: where it came from.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It didn't start with a stack. It started with two investor interviews, kept anonymous in our research notes. One of them summed it up:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;"I have no time to scout; if a tool pre-selects and I review each pick by hand, I've already won."&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Junction's Cala challenge, &lt;a href="https://junction2026.com/challenges/cala-make-unicorns-real" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Make Unicorns Real&lt;/a&gt;, asked for a product that solves a real need for founders or investors, powered by reliable public data. Slush started right after the hackathon. The brief said to aim for something they could start using the day after. That deadline became our constraint.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Third point: the team.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I brought the first idea, ran the interviews, sat in the Cala briefings, coordinated the team, built the web app and cut the pitch film. I didn't do it alone:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;José Javier Bravo Sarabia&lt;/strong&gt; built the discovery engine (the reverse walk, the second ring of co-investors, the proximity score) plus the shared cache, the PWA and the deploy.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Juan López Bravo&lt;/strong&gt; owned the product flow and research docs, the Cala client and cache, report polish, the guided demo and the peer-review script.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rubén Moyano Palazón&lt;/strong&gt; shaped the agent's questions and focus, added live voice transcription, and ran the engine review and data audit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Four people, each driving AI coding agents on separate branches. More than 100 reviewed pull requests. One weekend.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The two lessons I'm keeping
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lesson 1: if the question lives in a graph, don't read the big set in list order.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The naive way: go through Slush attendee by attendee. Cost: about &lt;strong&gt;9,000 Cala calls&lt;/strong&gt;, and you still miss most of the interesting companies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The way that worked: start from the investors you trust, walk their rounds, &lt;em&gt;then&lt;/em&gt; intersect with the event.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result on our real cache, with Y Combinator and 500 Global as the trusted pair:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Startups with a trusted investor in their rounds: &lt;strong&gt;5 → 27&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;8 of the top 10&lt;/strong&gt; in our ranking were invisible before.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reproducible from the repo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Same data. Different direction. Five times the signal.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lesson 2: an agent that invents a number is worse than no agent.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the model never touches the numbers:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ranking is &lt;strong&gt;deterministic&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Gemini writes &lt;strong&gt;only prose with placeholders&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The server fills every value from a sourced fact.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A missing field reads &lt;strong&gt;"Not published"&lt;/strong&gt;, never zero.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Trust has to be visible on the fact itself, not in a footnote at the end.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Live demo: &lt;a href="https://www.grabbit.cloud" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.grabbit.cloud&lt;/a&gt; (add &lt;code&gt;?demo&lt;/code&gt; for the button that stages an alert)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pitch video (2 min): &lt;a href="https://youtu.be/63DRjFkbU_A" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://youtu.be/63DRjFkbU_A&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Source (MIT): &lt;a href="https://github.com/JJavierBS/Grabbit" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/JJavierBS/Grabbit&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;  &lt;iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/63DRjFkbU_A" width="710" height="399"&gt;
&lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Try it in four steps, about a minute:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tap &lt;strong&gt;"AI startups at pre-seed and seed"&lt;/strong&gt;. Answer or skip the questions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F9oncvrr7dfjplf962ez8.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F9oncvrr7dfjplf962ez8.png" alt="Grabbit screenshot 1" width="800" height="500"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Grabbit checks the Slush attendees first, then asks only what the data can't settle: which investors you trust. (Guided demo, sample data.)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Type &lt;strong&gt;"I trust Y Combinator and 500 Global"&lt;/strong&gt;. The report opens, ranked by paths to those investors, with a source on every fact and a "Why it is here" path through the graph.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F41t0dj2t41xov65rxwpq.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F41t0dj2t41xov65rxwpq.png" alt="Grabbit screenshot 2" width="800" height="500"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Left: the path Grabbit walked in Cala's entity graph, step by step. Right: the report it wrote from it. (Guided demo, sample data.)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Type &lt;strong&gt;"Shorter, one line per startup"&lt;/strong&gt;. Watch it rewrite itself. The history keeps what changed, with undo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Press &lt;strong&gt;Alerts&lt;/strong&gt;. About thirty seconds later, an alert lands.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fylrxok5kjg7pelhn8c9n.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fylrxok5kjg7pelhn8c9n.png" alt="Grabbit screenshot 3" width="800" height="500"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;The loop closes: a startup on your shortlist moves and you hear about it. Labelled "Simulated event" on purpose. (Guided demo, sample data.)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One honest detail, because I'd want to read it in someone else's write-up: &lt;strong&gt;the demo alert is staged&lt;/strong&gt;, and it's labelled &lt;strong&gt;Simulated event&lt;/strong&gt; on screen. No startup raises money on cue. It still goes through the real diff, the importance check and the delivery path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The deployed API answers from a cached copy of Cala (&lt;code&gt;CALA_MODE=replay&lt;/code&gt;) so the demo survives venue wifi. Y Combinator and 500 Global are the warmed pair.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Want to run it yourself?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No keys:&lt;/strong&gt; replay mode plus scripted model answers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;With keys:&lt;/strong&gt; set &lt;code&gt;CALA_API_KEY&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;GEMINI_API_KEY&lt;/code&gt; and warm the cache once.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Partner Technologies
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Take Cala out of Grabbit and nothing is left.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The question Grabbit answers, &lt;em&gt;which startups at Slush are a few funding rounds away from the investors I trust?&lt;/em&gt;, lives in Cala's funding-round graph: who invested, who led, when, and according to which source.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How we used Cala
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We built on the raw entity endpoints, as Cala asked at the workshop, not on the LLM knowledge search they were retiring. Four calls carry the whole product:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Search entities&lt;/strong&gt; by name.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Introspect&lt;/strong&gt; a node.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Retrieve&lt;/strong&gt; a node and its neighbours.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Knowledge query&lt;/strong&gt;, only for news.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On 15 November our cache held &lt;strong&gt;1,145 Cala responses&lt;/strong&gt;: 484 funding rounds, 226 companies with properties, and the 2,989 Slush attendees.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What was great
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Rounds come with participants, roles and &lt;code&gt;is_lead&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Every fact can carry &lt;code&gt;source_rank&lt;/code&gt; and an honest date precision.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Events are entities, so we could start from the real attendee list.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What hurt, and what we did about it
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The data is strong. As a platform for a real-time product, it's still young.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No subgraph endpoint.&lt;/strong&gt; A first chat turn can mean hundreds of calls: &lt;strong&gt;898&lt;/strong&gt; in one measurement, mostly served from cache.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Undocumented rate limit.&lt;/strong&gt; Four parallel requests tripped a 429 with no &lt;code&gt;Retry-After&lt;/code&gt;. A single serial burst tripped it after about twelve calls.

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Fix:&lt;/em&gt; every request goes through one wrapper. One request in flight, 250 ms spacing, backoff, and a cache keyed by method, path and body, 404s included.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Outages.&lt;/strong&gt; On Saturday around 11:40 we saw 403s with an HTML body for several minutes. &lt;em&gt;Fix:&lt;/em&gt; we served from cache until Cala came back.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One fund, many entities.&lt;/strong&gt; A search for Y Combinator returned &lt;strong&gt;ten hits&lt;/strong&gt;: Company, Organization, Group, "Y Combinator W24"… most with no rounds. &lt;em&gt;Fix:&lt;/em&gt; a single fund-identity rule by id or name, used everywhere, because matching on identifiers alone wasn't enough.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;An edge you can't trust.&lt;/strong&gt; The direction of &lt;code&gt;INVESTED_IN&lt;/code&gt; was inconsistent. &lt;em&gt;Fix:&lt;/em&gt; we count only round participants.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The rest of the stack
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Around that core we used four more partner technologies, and we entered each of their tech challenges. They help, but they aren't the product:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Gemini&lt;/strong&gt; (Best Use of Gemini API) for chat with tool calls, self-rewriting report instructions, report prose, importance scores and live voice transcription. The model talks; it doesn't invent figures.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Aiven&lt;/strong&gt; (Best Use of Aiven): &lt;strong&gt;Aiven Runtime&lt;/strong&gt; hosts the API, and &lt;strong&gt;Aiven for Valkey&lt;/strong&gt; is the shared Cala cache, so warming the graph once served both our laptops and the deploy.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Context.dev&lt;/strong&gt; (Best Use of Context.dev) for unverified web signals, always labelled "web, unverified" and kept separate from Cala facts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ElevenLabs&lt;/strong&gt; (Best Use of ElevenLabs) for the sound of our pitch film: the orchestral music track, the sound effects on every cut, and the voiceover over the app captures. Not in the product. In the story we told about it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Hackathon Experience
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://2026.hackjunction.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Junction 2026&lt;/a&gt;: European Tech Renaissance. 13–15 November, Hype Areena, Espoo, Finland. About 2,000 builders, 48 hours, and partner challenges instead of a single prompt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We went as a team of four (José Javier, Juan, Rubén and me) for the Cala track, &lt;em&gt;Make Unicorns Real&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We're not a team that met on Discord the week before. José Javier, Juan and Rubén are former coworkers and people I knew from university. Above all, they're close friends. That's why we went together, and that's why we had a great time doing it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The weekend, in four beats:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Friday:&lt;/strong&gt; we committed to Cala as the verified source and to the raw entity API.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Saturday morning:&lt;/strong&gt; the persona locked in. A business angel, Slush as the starting list, a trust badge on every fact.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Saturday night:&lt;/strong&gt; the reverse walk landed, and suddenly the shortlist made sense.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Sunday:&lt;/strong&gt; review, fixes, a data audit on the real cache, the pitch video, and credit for the work that never shows up in &lt;code&gt;git log&lt;/code&gt;: the idea, the interviews, the briefings, the coordination.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The GitHub history is honest about the hour. My last commits on the pitch film and the team section landed after 02:00 on Sunday, Madrid time. Juan was still fixing the composer sizing in Safari around 03:00.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I won't invent a dramatic 3 a.m. outage I can't point to. The real breakage was slower and more useful:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;an API that's excellent at truth and awkward at volume,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a fund that turns out to be ten entities,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;venue wifi you shouldn't bet a live demo on,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;and a team that kept reviewing pull requests instead of merging on hope.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then Sunday's ceremony: &lt;strong&gt;third place in the Cala track.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But the result isn't what I'll remember first. It's the arena. Hype Areena was packed with talent, and the atmosphere was incredible. My favourite part: meeting so many Spaniards who, like us, took the risk of travelling that far to build for a weekend.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Want to go deeper?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The judge-facing version, with evidence next to every claim: &lt;a href="https://github.com/JJavierBS/Grabbit/blob/main/EVALUATION.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;code&gt;EVALUATION.md&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The engineering notes for Cala and for the next team that builds on their graph: &lt;a href="https://github.com/JJavierBS/Grabbit/blob/main/docs/building-on-cala.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;code&gt;docs/building-on-cala.md&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So, back to the question I started with: how many tabs did you open the last time you prepared for a conference?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With Grabbit the answer is zero. You talk to the report instead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Third place in the Cala track says the idea holds up. You decide whether the product does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're an investor who decides alone, or a builder who has ever drowned in tabs the night before a conference: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.grabbit.cloud" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the demo is live&lt;/a&gt;. Try it, then tell us in the comments where it's wrong.&lt;/strong&gt; That's the point of the agent: the more you talk to it, the shorter and more precise it gets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;José Javier, Juan, Rubén: see you in the comments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I rented an NVIDIA Blackwell pod on RunPod. Spun up the environment. PyTorch imported cleanly. torch.cuda.is_available() returned True. Everything looked fine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then I ran uvx computefence doctor.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;✗ NVIDIA RTX PRO 4000 Blackwell has compute capability sm_120, which is not supported&lt;br&gt;
by this PyTorch build (supports up to sm_90)&lt;br&gt;
Fix: Install a PyTorch build that supports sm_120: see &lt;a href="https://pytorch.org/get-started/locally/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://pytorch.org/get-started/locally/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Exit code 2. BLOCKER. Job stopped before it started.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What was about to happen&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The pod had an NVIDIA RTX PRO 4000 Blackwell compute capability sm_120. My installed PyTorch was 2.4.1+cu124, which only includes compiled kernels up to Hopper (sm_90).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;PyTorch doesn’t flag this at import time:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;import torch succeeds cleanly.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;torch.cuda.is_available() returns True because the GPU is visible and the CUDA runtime loads.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Your script downloads weights, tokenizes datasets, and warms up burning billable compute the whole time.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The crash only triggers when PyTorch executes its first CUDA kernel on a forward pass:&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;RuntimeError: CUDA error: no kernel image is available for execution on the device&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Pod still billing. Model not trained. No checkpoint saved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why torch.cuda.is_available() isn’t the right check here&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are four separate things that need to work:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GPU visibility&lt;/strong&gt;: Can the OS and NVIDIA tooling see the GPU?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;CUDA availability&lt;/strong&gt;: Can PyTorch initialise CUDA and access the device?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Architecture compatibility&lt;/strong&gt;: Does your PyTorch build contain compiled kernels for the GPU’s compute capability?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Kernel execution&lt;/strong&gt;: Can your training workload actually run on this hardware?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;torch.cuda.is_available() only confirms the first two. You can verify the mismatch manually:&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;python&lt;br&gt;
import torch&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;cap = torch.cuda.get_device_capability()&lt;br&gt;
print(f"GPU architecture: sm_{cap[0]}{cap[1]}")&lt;br&gt;
print(f"Supported by PyTorch: {torch.cuda.get_arch_list()}")&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;If sm_120 isn’t in that list, the job is a ticking clock.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why this is becoming more common&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;RunPod and Vast.ai are adding Blackwell nodes because they are fast and cost-competitive. Most existing Docker templates and install commands still pull cu118, cu121, or cu124 builds. None of those wheels contain sm_120 binaries Blackwell requires cu128 builds (PyTorch 2.7+ or nightly).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most people only discover the mismatch after the job crashes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How to fix it&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Force-reinstall PyTorch with a CUDA 12.8 wheel:&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;bash&lt;br&gt;
pip install --force-reinstall torch torchvision torchaudio --index-url &lt;a href="https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu128" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu128&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Use --force-reinstall a plain pip install torch often does nothing if the version number already appears satisfied.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Catch it automatically before the job starts&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Remembering to check get_arch_list() every time a provider assigns a newer GPU is easy to forget especially when is_available() already returned True.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;bash&lt;br&gt;
uvx computefence doctor &amp;amp;&amp;amp; python train.py&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;If computefence doctor exits with a non-zero code, train.py never runs. Two minutes fixing the wheel instead of 20 minutes paying to watch a model load and crash.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The pattern&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the same failure others have hit on Blackwell pods GPU assigned, older CUDA wheel installed, job dies at the first kernel with a cryptic error. PyTorch gives no warning until after you’ve paid for the warmup.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The check exists now. Run it before the job.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;pip install computefence · uvx computefence doctor&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Model drift ruins accuracy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Quick solution:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;acc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.75&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;revise_roadmap&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;When an AI feature performs well in testing but fails in production, aggregate accuracy metrics often mask underlying issues. A roadmap focused solely on feature delivery frequently misses the reality that model performance is a dynamic dependency. To build a resilient strategy, you must treat the data pipeline, evaluation, and monitoring as a unified lifecycle. As discussed in our guide on &lt;a href="https://raylabs.app/articles/ai-product-roadmap-data-readiness-a-strategic-guide/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AI Product Roadmap Data Readiness: A Strategic Guide&lt;/a&gt;, your roadmap must reflect the reality that data quality and availability are primary delivery constraints.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When model accuracy drifts, you must isolate the failure mode. For instance, if a photo-tagging model performs well on high-quality images but fails in low-light conditions, your roadmap should shift from general feature expansion to targeted data acquisition and retraining for those specific segments. This process requires rigorous adherence to established safety and performance standards, such as those outlined in the &lt;a href="https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;NIST AI Risk Management Framework&lt;/a&gt; to ensure your updates do not introduce new biases or regressions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When revising your roadmap, use the following decision framework to manage trade-offs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Strategy&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;When to Use&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Trade-off&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Recommendation&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Segmented Retraining&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Performance gaps in specific data slices&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Risk of catastrophic forgetting&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Prioritize for critical user segments&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Data Augmentation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Insufficient training samples&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Increased compute and labeling costs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Use for underrepresented classes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Guardrail Implementation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;High false-positive rates&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Slower feature delivery&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Mandatory for high-risk domains&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Canary Deployment&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Post-retraining validation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Complex infrastructure requirements&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Essential for production safety&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before finalizing your updated roadmap, ensure you have established clear release gates. These gates should be based on performance metrics rather than arbitrary calendar dates. If a model fails to meet the required accuracy for a specific segment, the roadmap must account for the time needed to source new data and re-validate the model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Verification Checklist:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Audit production data to identify specific segments where performance has drifted.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Define SMART objectives for each segment, such as achieving a specific accuracy threshold within a set timeframe.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Establish independent evaluation sets that remain separate from training data to prevent overfitting.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Implement automated monitoring to detect regressions early, as recommended by &lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/machine-learning/concept-model-monitoring" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Azure Machine Learning Model Monitoring&lt;/a&gt; to maintain long-term reliability.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Review the updated roadmap with stakeholders to ensure that data acquisition and validation milestones are clearly communicated as dependencies.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Feature matrices are the least useful format in tooling advice. Every tool claims the same rows, the ticks are self-reported, and the answer depends on a context the table cannot see.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So here is a different format: six questions. Answer them for the piece of work in front of you and the tool usually falls out. I will do the same for the six tools, and name the cases where each one is the wrong choice — including the tool I work on.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 1: Is this a picture or a behaviour?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the artefact is a picture that needs to be &lt;em&gt;reviewed&lt;/em&gt;, you need a design canvas. If it is a behaviour that needs to be &lt;em&gt;run&lt;/em&gt;, you need a runtime.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most arguments happen because a team asks one tool to be both. Figma is a picture tool with some behaviour features. GSAP is a behaviour library with no picture surface. Neither is broken; the expectation is.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 2: Does the animation need to know something at runtime?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The pointer position, the item count, the error, the user's plan, the viewport width, &lt;code&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the answer is no, a recorded format is cheaper and smaller. This is where Lottie wins and where a lot of teams over-engineer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the answer is yes, you need something that stores rules — a state machine, physics parameters, layout constraints — and you should stop evaluating playback formats.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 3: Is the artefact a character or an interface?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Characters want skeletal animation and mesh deformation: Spine, Rive.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Interfaces want layout, typography, accessibility and data: Exode, or an environment like it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A character inside an interface is the genuinely hard case, and it usually means two tools plus a shared state machine. Pretending one tool covers both is how you end up with a mascot that breaks at 768px.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 4: Who tunes the motion?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If an engineer tunes it, a library is fine and often better. No new format, no new tool, full control.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If a designer tunes it, they need a visual surface, and the output has to be consumable without a translation step. That is the handoff question, and it is organisational more than technical.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If &lt;em&gt;both&lt;/em&gt; tune it, you need the tuning values to live in one artefact both roles can read. That is the strongest argument for an open JSON format over a binary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 5: What has to ship?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This one is brutal and clarifying.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A prototype for a review:&lt;/strong&gt; Figma. Nothing else is worth the setup.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A marketing loop:&lt;/strong&gt; Lottie or a video. Tiny files, no state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A component in a React app:&lt;/strong&gt; whatever compiles to a component, or GSAP inside it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The same component on iOS and Android:&lt;/strong&gt; a format that compiles to multiple platforms, or you accept drift.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A game:&lt;/strong&gt; Spine, Rive, or your engine's animation system.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A whole interactive screen:&lt;/strong&gt; an environment that owns layout + logic + export.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 6: What is the cost of the file being closed?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A binary animation file cannot be reviewed in a pull request, merged, linted in CI, or edited by an agent. Sometimes that does not matter — a decorative asset is a decorative asset.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It matters when motion is part of your product surface and changes every sprint. Then version control stops working at exactly the moment you need it most, and someone starts attaching screenshots to PRs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The six tools, placed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Figma&lt;/strong&gt; — picture tool, best-in-class collaboration, weak runtime story. Use it for exploration, systems, review. Do not use it to specify behaviour and expect behaviour to arrive.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rive&lt;/strong&gt; — character and vector state machines with a small, proven runtime. Excellent when the artefact is art and the target is a canvas or a game. Weak when the artefact is a responsive interface with text and focus states.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lottie&lt;/strong&gt; — recorded playback, tiny files, universal support. Perfect for illustration. Fundamentally unable to react, so do not build interaction on segment scrubbing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GSAP&lt;/strong&gt; — imperative animation library, unmatched sequencing, no design surface. Ideal when motion is authored in code by the people who ship it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Spine&lt;/strong&gt; — game skeletal animation, deepest rigging, engine pipelines. Wrong shape for UI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Exode&lt;/strong&gt; — an environment that treats layout, physics, state and export as one artefact: flexbox canvas, hierarchical state machines, simulated motion, &lt;code&gt;.exode&lt;/code&gt; JSON, compiled components. Right when the whole screen is the deliverable. Wrong when you need one tiny asset, or when your team has no appetite for a new tool.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The combinations that actually work
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The best stacks I have seen are not monogamous.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Figma for exploration → Exode for the shipped screen.&lt;/strong&gt; Draw freely, then build the real thing once behaviour matters.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Spine for characters → Exode for the interface around them.&lt;/strong&gt; Shared state machine, two rigs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GSAP for page choreography → component-level motion in the design artefact.&lt;/strong&gt; Each tool does what it is best at.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Lottie for decorative assets → everything interactive elsewhere.&lt;/strong&gt; Resist the urge to make the illustration interactive.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The failure mode is not picking the "wrong" tool. It is picking four and making the integration everyone's side project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A note on switching costs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every migration costs more than the demo suggests: retraining, rebuilding libraries, re-earning trust. A tool has to remove a recurring, expensive problem to justify that. "It has more features" does not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The recurring problem worth paying to remove, in my experience, is the gap between a screen that was designed and a screen that behaves. If that gap is not expensive for you, you do not need a new tool. You need a better design review.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Priya works on design systems at Exode. If question 5 landed on "a whole interactive screen", that is the case Exode is built for — &lt;a href="https://exodeui.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;exodeui.com&lt;/a&gt;. If it landed anywhere else, use the tool that fits.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


OpenAI Just Shipped GPT-6.2 Astra and Sol, and the Cheaper Model Is the Interesting One
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;OpenAI launched GPT-6.2 Astra and GPT-6.2 Sol on Tuesday, November 17, 2026. Astra is the new frontier model, built for demanding professional and scientific work. Sol is the versatile everyday model, faster and far cheaper. Both are available now in ChatGPT Work and Codex for Plus, Pro, Enterprise and Edu users, and both are on the API as &lt;code&gt;gpt-6.2-astra&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;gpt-6.2-sol&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The headline most people will repeat is Astra: 20% cheaper than GPT-6 Astra, second on Artificial Analysis's Intelligence Index, and the first model OpenAI has ever released that it rates High for AI self-improvement. That's all real, and the safety rating deserves a serious look. But the model that actually changes what most developers should do is Sol — because on the coding benchmark FrontierCode 2.0, Sol trails Astra by just 0.5 points at one-fifth of the cost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That gap, 0.5 points for 80% less money, is the whole story. This is not a review — I have not had hands-on time with either model, and nobody outside OpenAI has had much. It's what OpenAI actually announced, what the numbers say so far, and the questions worth asking before you change anything in your workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What OpenAI actually launched
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two models, arriving together, aimed at different jobs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GPT-6.2 Astra, the frontier model.&lt;/strong&gt; OpenAI calls it the most capable model it has ever built for professional and scientific work, trained to handle professional conventions in fields like law, finance and accounting more reliably. It's already the default model for many OpenAI employees internally, for technical and non-technical work alike. On Agents' Last Exam, a benchmark of multi-step professional workflows, Astra scores 5.2 points above GPT-6 Astra at less than a third of the cost, according to OpenAI. On ExploitBench Internal Port, a cyber benchmark, it jumps 22 points over GPT-6 Astra — OpenAI calls it its most capable model ever for cyber.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GPT-6.2 Sol, the everyday model.&lt;/strong&gt; Compared with GPT-6.1 Sol, OpenAI says it is smarter, more efficient, and lets you complete more tasks on the same paid plan. It powers through multi-step agentic workflows, writes clearly, and is meant to be the model you reach for by default. The number that matters: on FrontierCode 2.0, which checks whether code changes meet the quality bar of real projects, Sol trails Astra by just 0.5 points at one-fifth the cost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ultrafast mode spreads wider.&lt;/strong&gt; Pro 500 subscribers get Ultrafast with either model. For the first time, Pro 200 subscribers get Ultrafast too, starting with Sol. Ultrafast is also available on the API for both models at launch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where you can use them:&lt;/strong&gt; ChatGPT Work and Codex only. This rollout does not add either model to regular Chat. Enterprise and Edu users need their workspace admin to switch them on. OpenAI has not mentioned free accounts or regional limits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The pricing, in plain numbers
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Model&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Input / 1M tokens&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Cached input&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Output / 1M tokens&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;GPT-6.2 Astra&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$8&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$0.40&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$40&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;GPT-6.2 Sol&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$2&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$0.10&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$10&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Astra's input and output prices are 20% below GPT-6 Astra's, and cached input is 60% cheaper. Sol keeps GPT-6.1 Sol's pricing exactly. Astra's list price now matches Anthropic's Claude Fable 5.2, which Anthropic cut by 20% a week earlier — the two frontier models are now priced identically, and OpenAI says Astra beats Fable on several tests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;OpenAI's framing is about cost per finished task, not cost per token: both models deliver better results with fewer output tokens, so you get more done inside the same usage limits. From December, that framing gets sharper — Plus, Pro and Business plans will share one allowance across ChatGPT and Codex, measured in tokens, so a model that wastes tokens will cost you allowance twice over.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Sol is the interesting one
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The frontier model always gets the launch-day attention. But look at the arithmetic from a developer's point of view.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you are running coding agents — Codex, or your own harness on the API — the question is never "which model is best?" It is "which model is good enough for this task, at what cost?" Sol at one-fifth of Astra's price, trailing by half a point on FrontierCode 2.0, is the kind of trade most teams should take for most tasks. The expensive model becomes the one you escalate to: the hard bug, the security-sensitive review, the multi-step professional workflow where Astra's 5.2-point lead on Agents' Last Exam earns its keep.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is also, pretty clearly, what OpenAI wants. Sol is the default-workhorse pitch: "super efficient and built to perform where it counts — the real world," in OpenAI's own words on launch day. The company is selling a two-model system, not a single flagship — Astra for the hardest work, Sol for everything else, with Ultrafast now reaching the cheaper Pro tier for the first time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The honest caveat: FrontierCode 2.0 is one benchmark, and it's OpenAI's chosen showcase for Sol. Independent harnesses will tell us whether that 0.5-point gap holds up on real repos. But the direction is unmistakable — the gap between the flagship and the cheap model has narrowed to the point where defaulting to the flagship is a habit worth questioning, not a decision.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The safety rating nobody should skip past
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Astra is the first model OpenAI has released that it rates High for AI self-improvement. The accompanying GPT-6.2 system card assesses both models as Critical for cybersecurity and High for biological and chemical capabilities under OpenAI's Preparedness Framework, with only Astra also meeting High for self-improvement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;OpenAI is launching both models with tighter safeguards for higher-risk cybersecurity assistance, and plans to offer them through OpenAI Daybreak, which already gives vetted security researchers access to other models with fewer guardrails.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is not a footnote. A model rated High for self-improvement, and Critical for cyber, is a model whose misuse case is real enough that OpenAI felt the need to say so in the launch announcement itself — on the same day it also boasted about Astra's 22-point jump on a cyber benchmark. The capability and the safeguard arrived in the same breath, and that is the right way to read them: the most capable cyber model OpenAI has ever shipped is also the one it is most careful about. If you build agents that touch code, credentials or infrastructure, that pairing is worth more of your attention than any benchmark score.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where the new models stand
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Artificial Analysis, which runs independent tests, ranks Astra second on its Intelligence Index at 62.1, behind Claude Fable 5.2 and ahead of Google's Gemini 4 Carbon and Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5. Sol scores 57.3 at max effort. Astra is up from 57 for GPT-6 Astra on the same index.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For context, this is a fast-moving family: GPT-6 Astra arrived in September, GPT-6.1 Sol followed a few weeks later at DevDay, and now both lines have moved to 6.2 together. GPT-6.1 Astra never shipped — OpenAI pulled it in September after it failed the company's safety bar — so GPT-6.2 is the first Astra upgrade since GPT-6 Astra itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Should you switch? A quick checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;If you use Codex or the API for everyday coding:&lt;/strong&gt; try Sol first. The 0.5-point FrontierCode gap at one-fifth the price is the best deal in this launch, if it holds up on your own work. Measure it on your own repo before you commit — one benchmark is not a migration plan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;If you do demanding professional work — law, finance, accounting, scientific analysis:&lt;/strong&gt; Astra is your model, and the 20% price cut plus fewer output tokens make it cheaper than the model it replaces. The Agents' Last Exam lead is the number to watch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;If you are on Pro 200:&lt;/strong&gt; you get Ultrafast for the first time, starting with Sol. That is a genuine upgrade to your plan, not just a new model name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;If you are on Plus:&lt;/strong&gt; both models are available in ChatGPT Work and Codex today, but check whether your workspace has them switched on — Enterprise and Edu need an admin to enable them, and rollout is still progressing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;If you build agents that touch production systems:&lt;/strong&gt; read the system card before you read the benchmarks. The High self-improvement rating and the tighter cyber safeguards are the part of this launch that will shape what you are allowed to build with these models.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What we still don't know
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;How the 0.5-point Sol gap holds up independently.&lt;/strong&gt; FrontierCode 2.0 is OpenAI's showcase; third-party harnesses have not weighed in yet.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Real-world token usage.&lt;/strong&gt; "Fewer output tokens" is OpenAI's claim; your usage limits will tell you the truth within a week.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;When — or whether — the models reach regular Chat.&lt;/strong&gt; This rollout is ChatGPT Work and Codex only.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What the December allowance change costs in practice.&lt;/strong&gt; One shared token allowance across ChatGPT and Codex changes how heavy users budget; the details will matter more than the announcement.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;How the tighter cyber safeguards behave in practice.&lt;/strong&gt; More capable cyber model plus more safeguards is a combination developers will test quickly, and the friction will show up in real workflows.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Our take
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;OpenAI's pitch this time is not "bigger model, bigger number." It is "the cheap model is nearly as good, and the expensive model is cheaper than it was." That is a pricing story dressed as a launch story, and it is the right story for where this market is: the frontier models are converging on capability, so the fight has moved to cost per finished task.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sol is the model to try. Astra is the model to escalate to. And the safety rating is the part of the announcement to take most seriously — the first High for self-improvement is a line being crossed in public, with safeguards attached, and the industry will be watching how that holds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Are you on Codex or the API today? If Sol really does trail Astra by half a point at a fifth of the price on your own work, would you switch your default — or is the flagship still worth it for the tasks you care about? I'm curious where your line is.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Sources: OpenAI, "GPT-6.2 Astra and Sol are now available" (community.openai.com, November 17, 2026); Thurrott, "OpenAI Launches GPT-6.2 Astra and Sol Models in ChatGPT Work and Codex" (November 17, 2026, &lt;a href="https://www.thurrott.com/a-i/344003/openai-launches-gpt-6-2-astra-and-sol-models-in-chatgpt-work-and-codex" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.thurrott.com/a-i/344003/openai-launches-gpt-6-2-astra-and-sol-models-in-chatgpt-work-and-codex&lt;/a&gt;); The Decoder, "OpenAI launches GPT-6.2 Astra and Sol with lower API prices and some benchmark gains" (November 17, 2026); Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index scores (Astra 62.1, Sol 57.3) as reported at launch. GPT-6.1 Astra history as reported by MadRobot. No hands-on testing was performed for this article — where a fact is OpenAI's claim, we say so. This breaking article is not the daily buyer-intent post, and it contains no affiliate links. AI disclosure: this article was drafted with AI assistance and checked against the OpenAI announcement and the sources above.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Also on ToolsDig: &lt;a href="https://toolscout-e2i.pages.dev/posts/gpt-6-2-astra-sol-models.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OpenAI GPT-6.2 Astra and Sol: the Cheap Model Is the Interesting One&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm Michael, a solo developer in Graz, Austria. I run SendVeil (&lt;a href="https://sendveil.link/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://sendveil.link/&lt;/a&gt;), a service for sending large original files through private, expiring links. This is a short write-up of the upload path, because "large files in a browser" turned out to be the hard part.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Parts, not one request
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every file is declared up front with its size and split into 64 MiB parts. Each part is uploaded as an R2 multipart part. The expected size of every part is computed on the server from the declared size, so a short or oversized part is rejected instead of silently corrupting the file. A 450 GB file is about 6,700 parts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


One Durable Object per workspace
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;State lives in a Durable Object per workspace, created with EU jurisdiction, backed by its SQLite storage. It records the multipart upload id once (a second caller gets the first id back, so a retry cannot start a parallel upload), the parts already accepted, and the file state (declared → uploading → complete). If the connection drops, the browser asks which parts exist and continues after the user selects the same file again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Downloads
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Recipients don't need an account. Downloads stream straight from R2 with HTTP Range support, so download managers and resumed downloads work, and the bytes are exactly what was uploaded: no compression or conversion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What it is not
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Transfers use HTTPS and R2 encrypts data at rest, but SendVeil is not end-to-end encrypted. I'd rather say that clearly than oversell it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Free plan gives 5 GB per transfer with 7-day retention and needs no card; paid plans go up to 450 GB per file. The UI is available in 20 languages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Questions about the Workers/R2 side are very welcome.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Michael&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How well do you actually know your favorite artist?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most of us listen to Spotify for hours every week. We swear we can identify a Vishal Dadlani bridge, a Arjit Singh synth intro, or a Kendrick Lamar beat drop in half a second flat. But when you strip away the track title, scramble the choices, start a 10-second countdown, and hit play on a random snippet... things get chaotic very fast.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That was the itch behind &lt;a href="https://soniq.dibbu.dev" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;soniq&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted to build a fast-paced, high-adrenaline music quiz where you pick any artist in the world get thrown into a rapid-fire round of 10 tracks, and see if your music memory actually holds up.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the story of how I built it, the tech stack behind it, the quirky hurdles of Spotify's audio preview ecosystem, and what it took to take it from a fun weekend prototype to a production-ready web app.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want to poke around the code or try it out:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Live App&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;a href="https://soniq.dibbu.dev" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;soniq.dibbu.dev&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Source Code&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;a href="https://github.com/DivyanshuLohani/soniq" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub - DivyanshuLohani/soniq&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fr71djgqiedk62cwcxex5.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fr71djgqiedk62cwcxex5.png" alt="soniq Hero Banner - Fast-paced artist quiz in action" width="799" height="448"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Core Concept
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The premise of soniq is dead simple:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pick an Artist or Playlist&lt;/strong&gt;: Search for anyone from Daft Punk to Juice Wrld or tap into your saved Spotify playlists.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;10 Rapid-Fire Clips&lt;/strong&gt;: You get 10 questions. Each question plays a short audio snippet.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Beat the Clock &amp;amp; Build Streaks&lt;/strong&gt;: You have 10 seconds per track. The faster you answer, the more points you bank. Answer consecutively to stack streak multipliers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Global Leaderboard &amp;amp; Challenge Links&lt;/strong&gt;: Compare scores against other fans worldwide, or generate a 1-click invite link to see if your friends can top your run.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No fluff, no 45-second onboarding videos. Just press play and test your ear.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Tech Stack Overview
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted this app to feel lightning fast, responsive on mobile, and resilient to third-party API quirks. Here's what powers it:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Frontend&lt;/strong&gt;: React 18, Vite, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, Lucide icons&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Audio Synthesis&lt;/strong&gt;: Native Web Audio API (for zero-latency UI sound effects and ticks)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Backend&lt;/strong&gt;: Express.js with TypeScript running on Bun / Node&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Database &amp;amp; ORM&lt;/strong&gt;: PostgreSQL paired with Prisma&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rendering &amp;amp; SEO&lt;/strong&gt;: Vite SSR + Static Pre-rendering generator + Open Graph tags&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;External APIs&lt;/strong&gt;: Spotify Web API + Apple Music / iTunes Preview fallback&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Designing the Experience: Retro Modern &amp;amp; Mobile-First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you play a trivia game on your phone and the layout jumps every time an album cover loads, you close the tab. Period.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Frzibu7hky6xddwccx19l.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Frzibu7hky6xddwccx19l.png" alt="Mobile UI Screen showing the 100dvh quiz player with countdown ring and answer buttons" width="433" height="964"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Big Engineering Hurdle: The Spotify Audio Preview Trap
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building a music guessing game sounds straightforward until you meet the Spotify Web API in production.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here’s the plot twist: &lt;strong&gt;Spotify has deprecated or removed 30-second audio preview URLs (&lt;code&gt;preview_url: null&lt;/code&gt;) for a massive chunk of their catalog.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you search an artist like XXXTentacion or Hanuman Kind on Spotify's endpoints today, you will often find that 8 out of 10 tracks return &lt;code&gt;preview_url: null&lt;/code&gt;. If you only rely on Spotify's raw preview field, your quiz either crashes or plays dead silence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The Solution: Dual Audio Resolution Pipeline
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To guarantee every quiz has playable, crystal-clear 30-second clips, I built an automated fallback pipeline:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;[User Selects Artist]
│
▼
[Fetch Top Tracks from Spotify API]
│
├─► Has valid preview_url? ──► YES ──► Use Spotify 30s MP3
│
└─► preview_url is null?
│
▼
[Query iTunes / Apple Music Search API by ISRC / Track Title]
│
├─► Matched Audio Preview? ──► Cache &amp;amp; Serve Apple Preview
│
└─► Still missing? ──► Silently discard &amp;amp; pick alternate track
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This ensures that whether a track is from 1975 or last week, the player gets instant, audible playback without buffering stutters.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Real-Time Audio Without Lag: Web Audio API
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When someone selects an answer, the feedback has to be instantaneous. Standard &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;audio&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; elements can introduce annoying micro-delays on mobile devices when triggering quick click sounds, streaks, or countdown beeps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of bundling heavy MP3 assets for UI effects, I wrote an in-memory &lt;strong&gt;Web Audio synthesizer&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;audioSynth.ts&lt;/code&gt;). It generates synthetic sine and square wave tones directly in the browser's audio buffer:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Lightweight, zero-latency feedback synthesized via AudioContext&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;playSound&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'correct'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'wrong'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'tick'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'streak'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getAudioContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;osc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;createOscillator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;gain&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;createGain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'correct'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Upbeat chime sequence&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;osc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;frequency&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setValueAtTime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;587.33&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;currentTime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// D5&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;osc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;frequency&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;exponentialRampToValueAtTime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;880.00&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;currentTime&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.15&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// A5&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Zero network requests, zero bundle weight, instant feedback.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Connecting Spotify Without Breaking Guest Flow
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Another tricky UX challenge was Spotify account connection.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In early builds, connecting Spotify felt clunky. If a user was already logged in as a guest, hitting "Connect Spotify" risked creating duplicate accounts or wiping their local leaderboard rank.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I refactored the auth flow to support &lt;strong&gt;progressive account elevation&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Anyone can immediately jump in as a &lt;strong&gt;Guest&lt;/strong&gt; and play right away.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;When you choose to connect Spotify, it securely links your Spotify OAuth tokens to your existing guest session via Prisma instead of resetting your progress.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Plus, we pull your actual public and private playlists so you can test yourself on your own curated rotation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Flx726j1wu6pg4qzixdjb.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Flx726j1wu6pg4qzixdjb.png" alt="Spotify Connect Modal" width="800" height="793"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ft3lnky2mib4xmkgyoss1.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ft3lnky2mib4xmkgyoss1.png" alt="Playlist select ui" width="430" height="663"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Static SSR Pre-rendering &amp;amp; SEO Optimization
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Single Page Applications (SPAs) are great for dynamic games, but notoriously bad for SEO and social sharing cards. When someone shares their high score link on Twitter or WhatsApp, you want a rich Open Graph preview with an image, title, and preview text—not a blank &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;div id="root"&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To solve this without having to migrate the entire app to a heavy server framework, I built a static pre-rendering pipeline into the build step:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Client Build&lt;/strong&gt;: Vite compiles the React app into &lt;code&gt;dist/&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Server Bundle&lt;/strong&gt;: Bun bundles the server entry into &lt;code&gt;dist-server/entry-server.js&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pre-render Script&lt;/strong&gt;: A custom script (&lt;code&gt;scripts/prerender.js&lt;/code&gt;) executes the React tree in Node/Bun for all major routes (&lt;code&gt;/&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;/leaderboard&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;/about&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;/terms&lt;/code&gt;, etc.) and injects the rendered HTML into static &lt;code&gt;.html&lt;/code&gt; files with dedicated Open Graph tags.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Client Hydration&lt;/strong&gt;: When a user opens the page, React smoothly hydrates over the pre-rendered markup.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


What it felt after a break
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building soniq reminded me of a few timeless truths about shipping side projects:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Third-party APIs will always change&lt;/strong&gt;: Never assume an external field (like &lt;code&gt;preview_url&lt;/code&gt;) will exist forever. Always build graceful fallbacks into your data pipeline.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Micro-interactions make or break a game&lt;/strong&gt;: Haptic feel, zero-latency audio chimes, and smooth countdown bars transform a basic quiz into something genuinely addictive.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Keep the friction near zero&lt;/strong&gt;: If playing requires 3 sign-up steps, 80% of users leave before their first track. Let people play instantly, and let them sign in when they want to save their high score.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What maybe I can impliment next:
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Real-Time Multiplayer Duels&lt;/strong&gt;: 1v1 Player match where you can battle against your friend and using sockets.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Custom Community Packs&lt;/strong&gt;: User-submitted playlists and curated decade challenges (80s Synthwave, 2000s Pop Punk, 90s Hip Hop).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Give &lt;a href="https://soniq.dibbu.dev" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;soniq.dibbu.dev&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; a spin, see how many tracks you can nail in 10 seconds, and let me know your high score!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;VoiceDash is the client portal I build for voice AI agencies: each agency's clients log in and see their own agent's calls, transcripts and analytics. For a long time those calls arrived two ways. Retell pushed them by webhook, and there was a "Sync Calls" button for everything the webhook missed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The button was the problem. Nobody presses a sync button on a dashboard they opened to read numbers. So I wrote a small React hook that syncs on its own, shipped it, and less than an hour later shipped a fix for a case I had not thought about: a tab you have not looked at yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is that hook, the one-line bug, and the parts I still have not fixed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the hook does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two client pages show calls: the conversation list and the analytics dashboard. Both now call the same hook with the agent's ID and a callback that reloads the page's data.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;SYNC_EVERY_MS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;60_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/** Returning to the tab syncs again unless the last sync was this recent. */&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;MIN_GAP_ON_RETURN_MS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;15_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCallSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;agentId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;undefined&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onSynced&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onSyncedRef&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;onSynced&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;onSyncedRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onSynced&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;agentId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;stopped&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;running&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lastRun&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// sync(), onVisibilityChange(), the timer and the cleanup go here&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;agentId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Three triggers: when the page opens, when the tab comes back into view (unless it synced in the last 15 seconds), and every minute while the tab is visible. The callback lives in a ref so the effect does not tear down and restart its timer every time the parent re-renders with a new arrow function. The effect depends only on &lt;code&gt;agentId&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;running&lt;/code&gt; flag stops a slow sync from overlapping with the next tick. &lt;code&gt;stopped&lt;/code&gt; stops a sync that finishes after the user navigated away from calling back into an unmounted page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why "only while visible"
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The server route behind the hook calls Retell's list-calls API with the agent's key. A dashboard left open in a forgotten tab for a weekend should not call a vendor API 2,880 times for nobody. So the first version guarded every sync the same way:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sync&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;running&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;visibilityState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"visible"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;setInterval&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;SYNC_EVERY_MS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That reads fine. It is also wrong for one case that showed up almost immediately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The background tab
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open a dashboard with a middle click. Or restore a browser session with five portal tabs. The page loads, renders the calls already in the database, and its &lt;code&gt;visibilityState&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;"hidden"&lt;/code&gt;. The opening sync returns early.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nothing is broken yet. The moment you switch to that tab, &lt;code&gt;visibilitychange&lt;/code&gt; fires and the hook syncs. But between load and switch, the page shows stale data while looking finished, and the dashboard's whole promise was "you never press Sync".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix was to treat the opening sync differently from the repeat:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sync&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;evenIfHidden&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;running&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;evenIfHidden&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;visibilityState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"visible"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;running&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;lastRun&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/api/retell/sync-calls"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"POST"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Content-Type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"application/json"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;agentId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Calls that arrived by webhook still show; the next sync retries.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;finally&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;running&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;stopped&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onSyncedRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;evenIfHidden&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;setInterval&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;sync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;SYNC_EVERY_MS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Note the &lt;code&gt;() =&amp;gt; sync()&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;code&gt;setInterval&lt;/code&gt;. Passing &lt;code&gt;sync&lt;/code&gt; directly would still work here because &lt;code&gt;setInterval&lt;/code&gt; calls it with no arguments, but the wrapper makes it obvious that the repeat never gets &lt;code&gt;evenIfHidden&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rule I took from it: a page someone opened is a request, even if they are not looking at it yet. A timer someone forgot about is not. Those deserve different guards, and I had written one guard for both.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Refreshing without flicker
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Syncing is half of it. After a sync the page has to show the result without becoming a loading skeleton every minute.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On analytics, the fetch takes a &lt;code&gt;quiet&lt;/code&gt; flag that skips the loading placeholders, plus a request counter so an old response cannot overwrite a newer date range the user picked meanwhile:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fetchData&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;quiet&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;latestRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;quiet&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setLoading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ... fetch /api/analytics&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;latestRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCallSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;agentId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetchData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The conversation list was harder because the user might have scrolled through three pages and opened a transcript. Replacing the list would throw that away. So the refresh fetches the newest page and merges: unseen calls go on top, calls already shown get their fields updated (a call stored mid-call gets its final duration and transcript later), and the list is re-sorted by start time. If the search box changed while the request was in flight, the result belongs to a different list and is dropped.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What makes it safe to call this often
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The client side is the easy part. The reason a once-a-minute sync is cheap lives on the server, and I covered most of it in &lt;a href="https://dev.to/nabeelbaghoor/a-webhook-tells-you-something-happened-not-that-you-have-everything-1fck"&gt;A Webhook Tells You Something Happened, Not That You Have Everything&lt;/a&gt;. The short version:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Each agent stores &lt;code&gt;callsSyncedUntil&lt;/code&gt;, how far its Retell history is known to be complete. A routine sync reads oldest first from two hours before that point, so it is usually a single request.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The watermark only moves forward. The update is conditional (&lt;code&gt;callsSyncedUntil: null&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;lt: until&lt;/code&gt;), so two syncs of the same agent racing each other cannot move it backward.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Inserts use &lt;code&gt;createMany&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;skipDuplicates&lt;/code&gt; against a unique key on &lt;code&gt;(agentId, platformCallId)&lt;/code&gt;. Two tabs syncing the same agent at the same second store each call once.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The route spends at most 25 seconds answering, then hands whatever is left of a long history to Next.js &lt;code&gt;after()&lt;/code&gt; with a bigger budget and a resume cursor.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Without those four, a polling hook on the client would be a load generator. With them, the hook is mostly a trigger.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I have not fixed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Honest list, from reading the code again for this post. None of these have shipped.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The hook ignores the response status.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;fetch&lt;/code&gt; only rejects on network failure. If the route returns 502 because Retell is down, or 400 because the key was removed, the hook treats it as a finished sync, calls &lt;code&gt;onSynced&lt;/code&gt;, and tries again in a minute. No backoff, and nothing on screen says the numbers are no longer being refreshed. The page just quietly stops changing. I would rather show a small "last synced 14 minutes ago" line than have someone trust a frozen dashboard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Every visible tab polls on its own.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;running&lt;/code&gt; is per hook instance. Two tabs on the same agent's analytics, both visible on two monitors, make two sync requests a minute. The server makes that safe, not free. A &lt;code&gt;BroadcastChannel&lt;/code&gt; so one tab syncs and the others just refresh would fix it. I have not needed it badly enough yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The list merge only looks at the newest page.&lt;/strong&gt; If a sync backfills calls from last month (a gap left by a broken webhook, say), they land in the database but not in a list you already scrolled, because the merge only fetches the first 50. They appear on the next full load. Rare, but it is exactly the case the backfill exists for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A first sync can hold the request for 25 seconds.&lt;/strong&gt; For an agent with no watermark, the opening sync reads Retell history up to the route's budget before answering. The page is usable the whole time, but the "new calls appear" moment can lag on a freshly connected agent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I would tell myself before writing it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Polling from the browser is a reasonable choice when the server work is idempotent and incremental. Most of my effort went there, not into the hook, and that was the right split.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hook itself needed exactly one more distinction than I gave it: who asked for this sync? The page opening, the user coming back, or a timer. Each one deserves its own guard, and collapsing them into a single &lt;code&gt;visibilityState&lt;/code&gt; check cost me a release.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you build portals for voice agents, this is part of what goes into &lt;a href="https://voice-dash.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;VoiceDash&lt;/a&gt;. And if you have solved the "one tab syncs, the rest listen" problem in production, I would like to know whether &lt;code&gt;BroadcastChannel&lt;/code&gt; held up or whether you ended up with locks in &lt;code&gt;localStorage&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On paper, building a resume editor sounds like a weekend project. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You throw together a two-column layout: form inputs on the left, a live preview on the right, and a shiny "Download PDF" button up top. Simple, right?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Except it almost never is. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The moment a user actually relies on your tool to apply for a job, expectations skyrocket. They expect the exported file to mirror the on-screen preview pixel for pixel. They expect it to fit neatly onto standard A4 paper without awkward blank gaps. They want the typography intact. And most importantly: they expect the output to be a &lt;em&gt;living, breathing document&lt;/em&gt;—one with selectable, copyable text that applicant tracking systems (ATS) can actually parse, and maybe even a Word (.docx) file they can tweak later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;While building &lt;strong&gt;Pagewise&lt;/strong&gt;, I quickly realized a fundamental truth that reshaped how I approached the entire architecture:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A document that looks great in a web browser isn’t guaranteed to survive printing, PDF parsing, or external word processors.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most web tools take the easy way out and treat resumes like glorified screenshots. We wanted Pagewise to treat a resume as a real document. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is how we tackled the engineering headaches behind making that happen.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


1. The A4 Problem: A Browser Is Not a Sheet of Paper
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Web browsers are built for fluid, responsive layouts. Documents are the exact opposite they are strictly bound by physical dimensions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An A4 page is strictly:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Width:&lt;/strong&gt; 210 mm&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Height:&lt;/strong&gt; 297 mm&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When you design a document editor purely using pixels or viewport percentages, you set yourself up for heartbreak. A layout might look gorgeous on a 1440p monitor, but the second the user tries to print or export it, things fall apart:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Browser print drivers apply arbitrary scaling.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Default margins eat away at your layout.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Sections awkwardly break right across the middle of a job title.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Content spills over onto a practically empty second page.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To solve this, we had to stop treating the canvas like a responsive webpage and start treating it as a physical medium. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our CSS establishes a rigid document boundary matching real-world paper dimensions:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.resume-page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;210mm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;min-height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;297mm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;box-sizing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;border-box&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;position&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;relative&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;background&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mh"&gt;#ffffff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;@page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;A4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;margin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.resume-page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;210mm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;min-height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;297mm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;margin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;box-shadow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;none&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Note: This is a simplified snippet, but it highlights the core mindset shift.&lt;/em&gt; &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Of course, CSS dimensions alone don’t magically solve pagination. When a user writes a lengthy work history, handling dynamic overflow without severing bullet points mid-sentence requires active content measurement and calculated page breaks.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


2. Typography Is Document Geometry, Not Just Decoration
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the sneakiest bugs we ran into had nothing to do with margins or flexbox it was fonts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In web design, a font change is mostly cosmetic. In fixed-geometry document design, &lt;strong&gt;fonts dictate layout math.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A subtle change in font weight, letter spacing, or line height can:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Alter exact line-wrapping points.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Expand a 2-line bullet point into 3 lines.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Increase section height by just 4 pixels... pushing an entire section onto an unwanted second page.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This becomes a nightmare when your frontend preview uses local system fonts or Google Fonts, while your backend PDF engine runs on a Linux server that has neither. When the server falls back to standard Helvetica or DejaVu Sans, your carefully calibrated single-page resume suddenly bleeds into page two.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


Our Font Normalization Matrix
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To combat this, we set up a controlled normalization strategy. Whenever a user picks a common font, our pipeline pairs it with verified, metrically close open-source equivalents across environments:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Requested Font&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Normalized / Open-Source Equivalent&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Georgia&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Lora / Liberation Serif&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Arial&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Arimo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Calibri&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Carlito&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Are they 100% identical down to the sub-pixel kerning pair in every environment? No. But this controlled mapping drastically reduces surprise layout shifts. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The takeaway:&lt;/strong&gt; If typography shifts between preview and export, your document geometry breaks. Font consistency must be treated as an infrastructure problem.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


3. The "Canvas Trap": Why We Refused to Flatten to Images
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you look at how many modern web apps generate PDFs, you'll see a very common shortcut:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run &lt;code&gt;html2canvas&lt;/code&gt; to take a screenshot of the DOM.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Slap that raster image inside a &lt;code&gt;jsPDF&lt;/code&gt; wrapper.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Call it a day.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It looks identical to the preview! It’s fast to build! &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;And it produces a terrible resume.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When you turn a document into a flat picture, you destroy its utility:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Screenshot / Canvas PDF&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;True Vector &amp;amp; Text PDF&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Text is baked into flat pixels&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Text remains sharp, selectable, and highlightable&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Copy-pasting text is impossible&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Recruiters can easily copy contact info or bullet points&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ATS parsers fail or require messy OCR&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ATS software reads native text streams directly&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Blurry when zoomed in or printed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Crisp vector rendering at any scale&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Zero screen-reader accessibility&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Screen-reader friendly&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine a recruiter trying to copy your email or phone number from a resume, only to find they're clicking on a JPEG. Or worse, an automated ATS pipeline rejecting your application because it couldn't extract a single keyword from an image.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At Pagewise, we committed to &lt;strong&gt;true vector text&lt;/strong&gt;. When you download a PDF, the text remains real, selectable text. While no format can guarantee 100% compatibility across every single outdated ATS parser in existence, outputting real text gives candidates the best possible shot.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


4. One Data Model, Two Completely Different Export Paths
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;People don’t just want PDFs. A huge chunk of users still need an editable Microsoft Word (&lt;code&gt;.docx&lt;/code&gt;) file—whether to submit through job portals that require it or to make custom adjustments for a specific application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The naive solution would be trying to convert the rendered HTML into Word, or exporting an image into a DOCX file. Both create a mess.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead, we decoupled our &lt;strong&gt;document state&lt;/strong&gt; from our &lt;strong&gt;rendering targets&lt;/strong&gt;. A single JSON resume model feeds into an export dispatcher with two dedicated pipelines:&lt;br&gt;
┌────────────────────────┐&lt;br&gt;
│  Resume Document Model │&lt;br&gt;
└───────────┬────────────┘&lt;br&gt;
│&lt;br&gt;
Export Dispatcher&lt;br&gt;
/ \&lt;br&gt;
/   \&lt;br&gt;
v     v&lt;br&gt;
┌─────────────────┐ ┌─────────────────┐&lt;br&gt;
│ Visual Pipeline │ │   python-docx   │&lt;br&gt;
│  (Vector PDF)   │ │  (XML-level)    │&lt;br&gt;
└────────┬────────┘ └────────┬────────┘&lt;br&gt;
│                   │&lt;br&gt;
v                   v&lt;br&gt;
┌─────────────────┐ ┌─────────────────┐&lt;br&gt;
│ Sharp, ATS-ready│ │ Native Editable │&lt;br&gt;
│   PDF Document  │ │   Word Styles   │&lt;br&gt;
└─────────────────┘ └─────────────────┘&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Path A (Visual PDF):&lt;/strong&gt; Renders the document model through a headless browser environment configured with strict print stylesheets, generating real text, vector borders, and predictable page breaks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Path B (Native DOCX):&lt;/strong&gt; Feeds the raw structured data into &lt;code&gt;python-docx&lt;/code&gt;. Instead of hacking HTML tags into Word, it builds real Word paragraphs, native headings, real bullet lists, and genuine XML table structures.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This means when a user opens the &lt;code&gt;.docx&lt;/code&gt; file in Microsoft Word, they aren’t fighting broken floating textboxes or glued-together layouts—they get a clean, standard Word document styled with native typography.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


5. The Hard Reality: Document Fidelity Is an Ongoing Battle
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Does having a single data model magically solve every cross-format inconsistency? &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Honestly? No.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Word and web browsers use fundamentally different layout engines. Word calculates line wraps, margin collapse, and paragraph spacing under its own rules. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A layout that comfortably leaves 15mm of breathing room at the bottom of an A4 PDF might push the last bullet point onto page two in Microsoft Word. A header accent bar that looks sleek in CSS requires a careful table border hack in DOCX XML.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Maintaining parity between on-screen previews, generated PDFs, and native Word documents isn’t a one-and-done feature. It’s an ongoing process of calibrating margins, tuning line heights, and testing edge cases.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


6. What Building This Taught Us
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building Pagewise forced us to look past the surface of web UI and think deeply about document architecture. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A resilient document editor has to separate three distinct layers:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Content:&lt;/strong&gt; The raw data (your jobs, dates, skills, metrics).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Presentation:&lt;/strong&gt; How that data looks right now on the screen.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Target Output:&lt;/strong&gt; How that data must behave inside an external ecosystem (A4 paper, Adobe Acrobat, Word, ATS scanners).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When you keep those concerns clean and distinct, the system stops being a fragile hack and starts behaving like a real document engine.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


What's Next?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We're continuing to refine Pagewise’s rendering engine to make multi-page handling and layout customization even smoother. Alongside the document pipeline, we've also introduced &lt;strong&gt;Wiser AI&lt;/strong&gt; a companion tool that helps analyze job descriptions, flag missing skills, and suggest targeted resume improvements without taking away user control.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want to see how the editor works in practice (or test the exports yourself), check it out here:&lt;br&gt;
👉 &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://pagewise.sadguruchenu.in/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Pagewise&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Let's Chat
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you’ve built document editors, invoice generators, or report exporters:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How do you bridge the gap between browser DOM previews and print/export parity?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Do you use headless browser rendering, native PDF libraries, or something else entirely?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd love to hear how other teams handle this in the comments!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The 3 AM Page: When Preview Chrome Becomes Render Artifact
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You wake to an alert: the automated video pipeline produced 47 corrupted exports overnight. Frame one shows your application's sidebar. Frame twelve captures a modal mid-transition. The encoder did exactly what you asked—it rendered the HTML you gave it, including the preview interface you forgot was part of the DOM tree.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the failure mode I am addressing while evaluating &lt;a href="https://github.com/heygen-com/hyperframes" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;heygen-com/hyperframes&lt;/a&gt;. Its premise, "Write HTML. Render video," makes HTML the production artifact. A preview dialog, responsive sidebar, or entrance animation must never quietly become part of that artifact.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a React application using Tailwind CSS v4 and Radix primitives, my recommendation is straightforward: &lt;strong&gt;isolate the render document, and animate only the surrounding interface&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


One Document, Two Consumers
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The architecture has three responsibilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application produces a self-contained HTML document. The preview interface displays that document without modifying it. A render adapter submits the same document, together with explicit viewport and timing settings, to the video-rendering workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That adapter is where Hyperframes integration belongs. I am not assuming a particular repository command, rendering API, or timing contract here; those require verification against the version being adopted. The implementation below deliberately stops at the document boundary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not export the React application root. That couples video output to navigation, portal placement, loaded application styles, and whatever animation happens to be running when capture begins.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead, treat the generated HTML as immutable input. Fonts, images, viewport dimensions, and the selected scene time are part of its rendering contract—not incidental browser state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why the Tutorial Version Breaks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A common preview implementation measures a panel with &lt;code&gt;getBoundingClientRect()&lt;/code&gt;, writes a new width, then repeats that process on the next animation frame. The read after the write can force synchronous layout. Add text wrapping and image decoding, and a smooth-looking demo becomes a source of dropped preview frames and unstable measurements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Moving the same animation into CSS does not automatically fix it: animating width or height still requires layout work. Use transforms and opacity for interface motion, and reserve fixed dimensions for the rendered document.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The related accessibility failure comes from treating a modal as an absolutely positioned &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;div&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. Adding &lt;code&gt;aria-modal="true"&lt;/code&gt; does not implement focus containment, Escape handling, or focus restoration. Keyboard navigation can continue behind the overlay while screen-reader users encounter a misleading dialog boundary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both failures come from giving preview chrome responsibilities it should not own.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A Reusable Preview Boundary
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In an existing React/TypeScript application configured for Tailwind v4, install &lt;code&gt;@radix-ui/react-dialog&lt;/code&gt;. Save this component as &lt;code&gt;VideoPreview.tsx&lt;/code&gt; and import the stylesheet below through your application entry point.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"@radix-ui/react-dialog"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VideoPreviewProps&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VideoPreview&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;html&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VideoPreviewProps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
